<ArticleLayout
  title="On Being Here"
  subtitle="A note on why I write"
  description="I have less than 100 years on this planet. Writing is how I make sense of what I see, think, and feel."
  date="2026-03-27"
  readTime="3 min read"
  tags={['Philosophy', 'Personal']}
>

  <p>
    I have less than 100 years on this planet. Probably much less. That's not a morbid thought — it's a clarifying one. It's the reason I started writing.
  </p>

  <p>
    For most of my life I consumed ideas without producing them. I read books, listened to podcasts, had conversations, and let the thoughts dissolve. I'd have an insight in the shower and forget it by lunch. I'd read something that changed how I saw the world and, six months later, struggle to remember what it was.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writing fixes that. When you write something down, you're forced to actually understand it. You can't hide behind vague impressions. The sentence either makes sense or it doesn't.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is this for?</h2>

  <p>
    Mostly for me. This is a personal archive — a record of what I think and learn as I move through the world. If I'm lucky, future-me will read these posts and be grateful that past-me wrote them down.
  </p>

  <p>
    But if someone else finds this useful, I'm genuinely happy about that. The best thing that can happen when you publish your thinking is that someone reads it and says: <em>I've been thinking about this too.</em>
  </p>

  <h2>What I'll write about</h2>

  <p>
    My interests don't fit neatly into one category. I studied physics. I work in banking. I spend a lot of time thinking about technology, consciousness, markets, and what it means to be alive in this particular moment in history.
  </p>

  <p>
    So that's what I'll write about. Technology, finance, philosophy — and whatever else I can't stop thinking about. No fixed schedule, no editorial calendar. Just notes, when I have something worth saying.
  </p>

  <h2>A note on quality</h2>

  <p>
    I'm not trying to write perfectly. I'm trying to write honestly. Some of these posts will be half-formed ideas. Some will be wrong. That's fine — I'd rather publish something imperfect than not publish at all.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    The perfect is the enemy of the good. And the good, published, is infinitely more useful than the perfect, sitting in a draft folder.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    If you disagree with something I've written, I'd genuinely like to hear from you. The <a href="/contact">contact page</a> is there for a reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    Welcome to strugen.com. Let's see where this goes.
  </p>

